<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ssec_sine_cosine" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
        <title>The Sine and Cosine Functions</title>
        <introduction>
                                        <p>
                                                        Probably the two most important functions in trigonometry are defined based on angles and the coordinates of points on a circle of radius <m>1</m>, which we refer to as <term>the unit circle</term>.
                                        </p>
                                        <p>
                                                        For any point, <m>P</m>, on the unit circle, we measure the angle for <m>P</m> <alert>in radians</alert> from the positive <m>x</m>-axis counter-clockwise to <m>P</m>.  Negative angles and angles greater than <m>2\pi</m> are allowed: negative angles are measured counter-clockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis, and angles greater than <m>2\pi</m> (or less than <m>-2\pi</m>) start around the circle again.                                        </p>
                                        <figure xml:id='fig_unit_circle_theta'>
                                                <caption>The Unit Circle with an Angle Shown</caption>
                                                <image width='50%'>
                                                        <description><p>The Unit Circle with an Angle and a Point Shown</p></description>
                                                        <latex-image>
                                                                \begin{tikzpicture}\scriptsize
                                                                \draw (0,0) circle (1)
                                                                (-1.2,0) -- (1.2,0)
                                                                (0,-1.2) -- (0,1.2)
                                                                ;
                                                                \fill ( 0,0) circle (0.025);
                                                                \fill (50:1) circle (0.025);
                                                                \draw (0,0) node[below left] {\((0,0)\)}
                                                                -- (50:1) node[above right] {\(P=(a,b)\)};
                                                                \draw[->] (0.4,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=50, radius=0.4];
                                                                \draw (25:0.5) node {\(\theta\)};
                                                                \end{tikzpicture}
                                                        </latex-image>
                                                </image>
                                        </figure>
                                                <p>
                                                        <xref ref='fig_unit_circle_theta'/> shows the unit circle with a point <m>P=(a,b)</m> and its angle, <m>\theta</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
                        Now that we have the unit circle and have explained how we measure angles on the unit circle, we are ready to define the circular functions <term>sine</term> and <term>cosine</term>.
        </p>
        <definition>
                <title>The Sine and Cosine Functions</title>
                <statement>
                        <p>
                                For any real number <m>\theta</m>, define the point <m>P=(a,b)</m> on the unit circle whose angle (measured from the positive <m>x</m>-axis) is <m>\theta</m><nbsp/>rad. Then the cosine of <m>\theta</m> is the <m>x</m>-coordinate of <m>P</m> and the sine of <m>\theta</m> is the <m>y</m>-coordinate of <m>P</m>. That is,
                                <md><mrow>\cos(\theta)&amp;=a, &amp;\sin(\theta)&amp;=b.</mrow></md>
                        </p>
                </statement>
        </definition>
        <example xml:id='example_45deg'>
                <statement>
                        <p>
                                        We will find <m>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)</m> and <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                                We know that <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> is <m>45^{\circ}</m>, so we start by drawing a point on the unit circle that makes an angle of <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> with the positive <m>x</m>-axis and drop a perpendicular from the point to the <m>x</m>-axis.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                                        Because we now have a <m>45,45,90</m> triangle, we know the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates of <m>P</m> will be the same size, and because we are in the first quadrant, they will both be positive. So, let <m>P=(a,a)</m>, where <m>a>0</m>, and we will find the value of <m>a</m>, and therefore the sine and cosine of <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
                        </p>
                                <image width='40%'>
                                                        <description><p>The Unit Circle with an Angle, a Point, and a Segment to the x-axis Shown</p></description>
                                        <latex-image>
                                                \begin{tikzpicture}\scriptsize
                                                \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                                                \draw (1.2,0) -- (0,0) -- (45:1);
                                                \draw (-1.2,0) -- (0,0) -- (0,1.2)
                                                (0,0) -- (0,-1.2);
                                                \fill (45:1) circle (0.02) node[above right] {\(P\)};
                                                \draw[dashed]
                                                let \p1 = (45:1)
                                                in
                                                (\p1) -- (\x1,0)
                                                ;
                                                \end{tikzpicture}
                                        </latex-image>
                                </image>
                        <p>
                                From the Pythagorean theorem, since the unit circle has radius <m>1</m> and the radius of the unit circle is the hypotenuse of the right triangle, we have 
                                <md>a^{2}+a^{2}=1^{2}</md>
                                so
                                <md>2a^{2}=1.</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                                        Dividing by <m>2</m> gives us
                                        <md>a^{2}=\frac{1}{2},</md>
                                        and then taking the (positive) square root give us
                                        <md>
                                                <mrow>a&amp;=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}</mrow>
                                                <mrow> &amp;=\frac{\sqrt{1}}{\sqrt{2}}</mrow>
                                                <mrow> &amp;=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}.</mrow>
                                        </md>
                                        If we want to rationalize the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by <m>\sqrt{2}</m> to get
                                        <md>
                                                <mrow>a&amp;=\frac{1\cdot\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{2}}</mrow>
                                                <mrow> &amp;=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}.</mrow>
                                        </md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                                        Therefore, we now know that <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m> and <m>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.
                        </p>
                </statement>
        </example>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
                <introduction>
                        <p>
                                In the following explorations, use symmetry, the Pythagorean theorem, and geometry to relate angles and the values of sine and cosine.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                                        One geometry fact you may not know is the following: in a triangle with angles
                                <m>\frac{\pi}{6}=30^{\circ}</m>,
                                <m>\frac{\pi}{3}=60^{\circ}</m>,
                                and
                                <m>\frac{\pi}{2}=90^{\circ}</m> (a 30-60-90 triangle) , the shortest side (opposite the <m>30^{\circ}=\frac{\pi}{6}</m> angle) is half the length of the hypotenuse (longest side).
                        </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                                Find <m>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)</m>
                                                and <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)</m>.
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                        <answer>
                                <p>
                                        <md>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}</md>
                                        <md>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</md>
                                </p>
                        </answer>
                        <solution>
                                <p>
                                        Dropping a perpendicular from the point at <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m> on the unit circle creates a 30-60-90 triangle with the perpendicular being the shortest side. Since the hypotenuse has length <m>1</m>, that means that the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the point is <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, and so <m>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}</m>. Then, from the Pythagorean theorem, if we let the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the point be <m>a</m>, we have
                                        <md>a^{2}+\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}=1^{2}</md>
                                        so
                                        <md>a^{2}+\frac{1}{4}=1</md>
                                                and therefore <m>a^{2}=\frac{3}{4}</m>. Since we know that <m>a>0</m>, we know that <m>a=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. Thus, <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>.
                                </p>
                        </solution>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                                Find <m>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)</m>
                                                and <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)</m>.
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                        <hint>
                                <p>
                                        Use symmetry and the previous problem.
                                </p>
                        </hint>
                        <answer>
                                <p>
                                        <md>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</md>
                                        <md>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}</md>
                                </p>
                        </answer>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                                Find <m>\sin\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)</m>
                                                and <m>\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)</m>.
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                        <answer>
                                <p>
                                        <md>\sin\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}</md>
                                        <md>\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}</md>
                                </p>
                        </answer>
                        <solution>
                                <p>
                                        Dropping a perpendicular from the point at <m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m> on the unit circle creates a 30-60-90 triangle with the perpendicular being the shortest side. Since the hypotenuse has length <m>1</m>, that means that the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the point is <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, and so <m>\sin\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}</m>. Then, from the Pythagorean theorem, if we let the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the point be <m>a</m>, we have
                                        <md>a^{2}+\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2}=1^{2}</md>
                                        so
                                        <md>a^{2}+\frac{1}{4}=1</md>
                                                and therefore <m>a^{2}=\frac{3}{4}</m>. Since the point is in the second quadrant, we know that <m>a\lt 0</m>, so we know that <m>a=\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. Thus, <m>\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>.
                                </p>
                        </solution>
                </task>
                <task>
                        <statement>
                                <p>
                                        Use symmetry to find the coordinates of each of the points at the important angles shown in <xref ref='fig_unit_circle'/>.
                                </p>
                        </statement>
                        <answer>
                                                <image width='50%'>
                                                        <description><p>The Unit Circle with an angle and associated point shown.</p></description>
                                                        <latex-image>
                                                                \begin{tikzpicture}\scriptsize
                                                                \draw (0,0) circle (3)
                                                                (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0)
                                                                (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4)
                                                                ;
                                                                \foreach \ang in {
                                                                2,3,4,6,
                                                                8,9,10,12,
                                                                14,15,16,18,
                                                                20,21,22
                                                                }{
                                                                \draw (15*\ang:2.91) -- (15*\ang:3.09)
                                                                ;
                                                                }
                                                                \draw
                                                                (  0:3.9) node[below right] {\( 0=2\pi, (1,0)                                                        \)}
                                                                ( 30:3.9) node              {\( \frac{\pi}{6}, \left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right)           \)}
                                                                ( 45:3.9) node              {\( \frac{\pi}{4}, \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)    \)}
                                                                ( 60:3.9) node              {\( \frac{\pi}{3}, \left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)           \)}
                                                                ( 90:3.9) node              {\( \frac{\pi}{2}, (0,1)                                                 \)}
                                                                (120:3.9) node              {\(\frac{2\pi}{3}, \left(\frac{-1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)          \)}
                                                                (135:3.9) node              {\(\frac{3\pi}{4}, \left(\frac{-\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)   \)}
                                                                (150:3.9) node              {\(\frac{5\pi}{6}, \left(\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right)          \)}
                                                                (180:3.9) node[below left]  {\(           \pi, (-1,0)                                                \)}
                                                                (210:3.9) node              {\(\frac{7\pi}{6},  \left(\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{-1}{2}\right)        \)}
                                                                (225:3.9) node              {\(\frac{5\pi}{4},  \left(\frac{-\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{-\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) \)}
                                                                (240:3.9) node              {\(\frac{4\pi}{3},  \left(\frac{-1}{2},\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)        \)}
                                                                (270:3.9) node              {\(\frac{3\pi}{2},  (0,-1)                                               \)}
                                                                (300:3.9) node              {\(\frac{5\pi}{3},  \left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)         \)}
                                                                (315:3.9) node              {\(\frac{7\pi}{4},  \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{-\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)  \)}
                                                                (330:3.9) node              {\(\frac{11\pi}{6}, \left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac{-1}{2}\right)         \)}
                                                                ;
                                                                \end{tikzpicture}
                                                        </latex-image>
                                                </image>
                        </answer>
                </task>
        </exercise>
                                        <figure xml:id='fig_unit_circle'>
                                                <caption>The Unit Circle with Important Angles</caption>
                                                <image width='50%'>
                                                        <description><p>The Unit Circle with Important Angles</p></description>
                                                        <latex-image>
                                                                \begin{tikzpicture}\scriptsize
                                                                \draw (0,0) circle (1)
                                                                (-1.2,0) -- (1.2,0)
                                                                (0,-1.1) -- (0,1.1)
                                                                ;
                                                                \foreach \ang in {
                                                                2,3,4,6,
                                                                8,9,10,12,
                                                                14,15,16,18,
                                                                20,21,22
                                                                }{
                                                                \draw (15*\ang:0.97) -- (15*\ang:1.03)
                                                                ;
                                                                }
                                                                \draw
                                                                (30:1.3) node {\(\frac{\pi}{6}\)}
                                                                (45:1.3) node {\(\frac{\pi}{4}\)}
                                                                (60:1.3) node {\(\frac{\pi}{3}\)}
                                                                (90:1.3) node {\(\frac{\pi}{2}\)}
                                                                (120:1.3) node {\(\frac{2\pi}{3}\)}
                                                                (135:1.3) node {\(\frac{3\pi}{4}\)}
                                                                (150:1.3) node {\(\frac{5\pi}{6}\)}
                                                                (180:1.3) node {\(\pi\)}
                                                                (210:1.3) node {\(\frac{7\pi}{6}\)}
                                                                (225:1.3) node {\(\frac{5\pi}{4}\)}
                                                                (240:1.3) node {\(\frac{4\pi}{3}\)}
                                                                (270:1.3) node {\(\frac{3\pi}{2}\)}
                                                                (300:1.3) node {\(\frac{5\pi}{3}\)}
                                                                (315:1.3) node {\(\frac{7\pi}{4}\)}
                                                                (330:1.3) node {\(\frac{11\pi}{6}\)}
                                                                ;
                                                                \end{tikzpicture}
                                                        </latex-image>
                                                </image>
                                        </figure>
</worksheet>

